Osprey kernel policy layer

This directory contains the portable portion of the Osprey kernel.  It is
kept independent of Proc, Mach, device drivers, and architecture trap code so
that the scheduling and queue contracts can be tested before integration.
This directory is the source-of-truth portable Osprey layer.  The top-level
Osprey directory contains only target build output, such as
Osprey/amd64/lib.

edf.c implements the first policy slice:

* resource-container CPU budget, period, deadline, and accounting fields;
* bounded memory, network-byte, and network-packet accounting and release;
* intrusive earliest-deadline-first task ordering;
* deterministic sequence ordering for equal deadlines.

queue.c implements a fixed-size single-producer/single-consumer request ring.
The architecture adapter must provide the memory barriers required when the
ring is shared across cores.

core.c adds one independent EDF run queue per core.  It deliberately
does not migrate tasks or touch Plan 9 Proc objects yet.  syscall.h reserves
the request/reply operation numbers for the asynchronous syscall adapter.

process.c groups those pieces into the first Osprey process contract: a
resource container, schedulable task, request ring, and reply ring.  The next
kernel adapter can attach this contract to a native process without exposing
the queue representation to the scheduler.

syscall.h defines asynchronous operations for file I/O, IPC, metadata,
namespace binding, allocation, yielding, and polling.  Requests carry async,
continuation, and reply flags; replies preserve status and flags while the
architecture adapter decides whether a blocked request needs a trap or an
inter-core wakeup.

ns.h and ns.c provide a bounded, allocation-free private namespace table for
container-local names.  The native adapter supplies the table storage and
performs the actual Chan binding; the portable layer only maintains the
local-to-global mapping.

snap.h and snap.c capture and restore portable process metadata for
checkpoint/restart.  Queue storage, user memory, and device handles remain
owned by the adapter; the snapshot only records state that is valid across a
restart or migration.

net.h and net.c provide the first packet-path primitive: fixed packet
descriptors and a bounded queue that charges bytes and packets to the owning
resource container.  Descriptors carry the shared driver-layer Block type,
allowing zero-copy handoff between device Chan code and Osprey scheduling.

runtime.c adds the flavor-independent kernel-side control plane.  It owns a
set of independent per-core schedulers, attaches and detaches processes, and
services one queued request at a time through a caller-supplied handler.  It
also exposes block, wake, exit, and conservative load-balancing operations.
The handler is where a native kernel or buddy operating system implements
file, IPC, and network operations; the scheduler and queue code do not know
about those services.

The runtime is deliberately storage-oriented: callers provide the core and
process arrays, and no allocation or platform-specific lock is performed.
An architecture adapter is responsible for interrupts, memory barriers,
address-space setup, and migration between cores.

The next layer follows the traditional port interface: Chan, device tables,
portdat.h, portfns.h, and error.h.  Architecture code supplies only trap,
MMU, interrupt, and timer details.  Osprey services should be exposed as
Inferno devices and Chan operations wherever possible, so native and hosted
adapters share the same contracts.

The initial port headers are based on the Inferno driver interface.  Osprey
lib.h supplies the common libc, Qid, Dir, and open-mode definitions expected
by that interface.  Osprey
uses the conventional Queue name for its asynchronous message ring; the
driver and qio layers are shared by the flavor rather than duplicated under
an Osprey namespace.  The 9front adapter can extend these headers where its
driver ABI differs without changing the portable Osprey policy files.

lib/mkfile builds these objects as libosprey.a.  It is intentionally separate
from the 9front libraries; the 9front kernel does not link or include this
library.

The next integration layer will adapt these objects to 9front Proc/Mach state,
then add per-core run queues and asynchronous syscall completion handling.
